```
Simpler: a `attempt` counter state; effect depends on attempt, and error retry increments attempt after setting state to loading. That avoids overlapping timers since effect cleanup clears previous timer.

```tsx
const [attempt, setAttempt] = useState(0);
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = window.setTimeout(... , 120);
  return () => window.clearTimeout(timer);
}, [attempt]);

const retry = () => {
  setState('loading');
  setAttempt(a => a + 1);
};
```
But repeated activation: button only rendered in error state, and once clicked state becomes loading → button disappears, so no overlapping timers. Also cleanup clears timer on attempt change. Good.

But careful: on re-run effect with new attempt, the old effect cleanup runs clearing old timer — fine.

Also the effect reads URL search param at completion time; on retry, the URL is still ?sync=error, so it would go back to error! Requirement: "Activating it immediately returns the panel to Checking service…, then completes as All changes are synced." So retry must force success. So capture requested on first run, and after retry force success.

Design: track `forceSuccess` ref/state? Use attempt: 
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const requested = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('sync');
  const next: State = attempt === 0 && requested === 'error' ? 'error' : 'success';
  ...
}, [attempt]);
```
That's clean and testable.

Retry button inside error div with role alert. Having a button inside role="alert" is okay-ish but better place button after the alert content. Keep role alert on the message div. Let's restructure:

```tsx
{state === 'error' && (
  <div className="sync-state error">
    <div role="alert">
      <strong>Sync failed</strong>
      <span>Check your connection and try again later.</span>
    </div>
    <button type="button" className="sync-retry" onClick={retry}>Retry sync</button>
  </div>
)}
```
Hmm, role="alert" originally wrapped both strong and span. Keeping role="alert" on outer div with button inside is acceptable but nested interactive in alert isn't ideal. The criterion "the error state includes a button named Retry sync". Tests may query getByRole('alert') and check text. Safer: keep outer div className="sync-state error" with role="alert" and put button inside — text content unchanged plus button. That preserves existing structure maximally. role="alert" is a live region; buttons inside are fine for tests. I'll keep outer role="alert".

Styling: .sync-state.error is `display: grid; gap: 4px`. Button will be full-width-ish? Grid items stretch; button would stretch full width. Add `.sync-retry` maybe `justify-self: start;` and margin-top. Use existing button styles, add small stylesheet rule:

```css
.sync-state.error .sync-retry { justify-self: start; margin-top: 8px; }
```
Actually simpler: add class `sync-retry` with those. I'll add to styles.css.

